# Density: the measurement that tells things apart when weighing cannot
#
# Put five grams of copper, five grams of zinc and five grams of aluminium
# on a balance and you learn nothing: they all weigh five grams. What
# separates them is how much ROOM those five grams take up, and that is
# density — mass divided by volume, a property of the substance itself
# rather than of the piece you happen to be holding.
register lv2

# 1. Three metals, the same mass, three different answers.
add v1 Cu 5g
measure v1 balance
measure v1 density

new beaker
add v2 Zn 5g
measure v2 balance
measure v2 density

new beaker
add v3 Al 5g
measure v3 balance
measure v3 density

# The balance says 5 g three times. The density meter says 8.96, 7.14 and
# 2.70 g/mL — and the aluminium piece is more than three times the size of
# the copper one. That is why a dealer weighs scrap metal AND checks how
# big it is.

# 2. Liquids have densities too, and dissolving something changes them.
#    This is what a density tower is built on: each layer has to be
#    heavier than the one above it or the tower will not stack.
new beaker
add v4 water 100mL
measure v4 density

new beaker
add v5 water 100mL
add v5 sucrose 50g
measure v5 density

new beaker
add v6 water 100mL
add v6 sucrose 150g
measure v6 density

# Three liquids, three densities, and they climb with the sugar. Poured
# very gently, heaviest first, they would sit in layers — but this bench
# has no slow pour: pour them together here and they mix, which is what
# would really happen if you poured them quickly. The numbers are the part
# the tower depends on, and they are right.

# 3. A density belongs to ONE substance. Two powders in a heap have a mass
#    and a volume, and no density anyone should be told.
new beaker
add v7 Cu 5g
add v7 Zn 5g
measure v7 density

# What this does not model: the volume of a piece is computed from the
# substance's reviewed density, not measured by displacing water, so this
# is the density of the material and not of the object — a hollow or
# bubbled piece would read the same here and float in a real bucket. The
# solute volumes in the sugar solutions use the additive-volume
# approximation the rest of the bench uses; a real syrup contracts very
# slightly on mixing.
